jt-pve-storage-synology (0.2.1~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Synology::Naming: name building and the ownership gate, which takes the
    storage id rather than trusting the shape of a name.
  * Snapshot names need no folding: DSM stores PVE's names exactly, and refuses
    a duplicate within one LUN with 18990513.
  * The naming functions refuse a method call instead of silently answering
    "not owned" when their arguments have shifted.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 21:45:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.2.0~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The first code that talks to a NAS: Synology::API, ::LUN and ::Target, with
    91 unit tests and a full lifecycle driven against a DS918+ on DSM 7.1.1.
    There is still no PVE plugin; this is the layer beneath it.
  * Fixed a lookup by id that reported "not there" when it had actually failed:
    target_id must be sent as a JSON string (a bare number earns 18990710), and
    the fallback searched by name only, so every mapping check silently answered
    no. Found by driving the code at hardware rather than by reading it.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 21:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.1.4~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * WWID derivation confirmed on a second independent sample, against a LUN
    attached to a Proxmox VE node.
  * A device is addressed by /dev/disk/by-id/dm-uuid-mpath-<wwid>, not
    /dev/mapper/<wwid>: a node with user_friendly_names yes names the map mpathX
    and that path does not exist.
  * Capacity never comes from summing allocated_size — a clone is a reflink that
    reports 512 MiB allocated while consuming zero bytes of the volume.
  * There is no built-in multipath configuration for Synology, so the conf.d
    drop-in is mandatory: without it the generic defaults apply, including
    no_path_retry "queue".
  * The documentation site defaults to English and carries the language in the
    URL as ?lang=zh, applied in <head> so there is no flash.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.1.3~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Rollback is supported: restore_snapshot(src_lun_uuid, snapshot_uuid) leaves
    the LUN uuid unchanged and keeps snapshots newer than the restored one, so
    volume_rollback_is_possible does not need the refusal the related projects
    require.
  * Both Synology high-availability arrangements are supported — SHA's floating
    cluster IP and UC/SA's per-controller addresses. Neither is verified on
    hardware; DSM UC produces a warning, not a refusal.
  * Hardware findings that change the design: a create reporting failure can
    create the LUN anyway; mapping_index is reused, so device identity must come
    from the kernel's WWID and never from a path; nothing refuses a delete for
    dependency reasons, including a mapped LUN.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.1.2~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix a published SHA256SUMS that could not be verified. GitHub rewrites the
    "~" in a release asset's filename to ".", so the checksum file named a file
    nobody could download and `sha256sum -c` answered "No such file or
    directory". The release workflow now renames the package to the name GitHub
    will serve before hashing it, and verifies its own checksum file rather than
    assuming. The .deb itself was always correct: dpkg reads the version from
    the control data, never from the filename.
  * Add CHANGELOG.md and CHANGELOG_zh-TW.md; release-check now requires an entry
    for the version being released.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 16:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.1.1~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The snapshot restore method is named restore_snapshot, established against
    hardware. Nine candidate names were asked about; one answered. Rollback is
    still refused in this release: knowing a method exists is not knowing its
    parameters or whether it keeps newer snapshots and preserves the LUN uuid.
  * The LUN listing no longer sends a types filter. Synology's own CSI driver
    sends an explicit twelve-type list, and on the test NAS that list hid a
    Virtual Machine Manager virtual disk whose 120 GiB comes out of the same
    volume. The probe now lists unfiltered and reports what the filter would
    have hidden.
  * The register no longer reports a listing that was skipped as one the
    account could not read. Skipped, refused and succeeded are three different
    outcomes, and conflating the first two is the mistake this project's own
    rules exist to prevent.
  * A read method that succeeds is no longer flagged as alarming; only a
    destructive candidate succeeding is.
  * Error 18990004 recorded, observed from SYNO.Core.ISCSI.Host get.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 15:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.1.0~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * First packaged build. Contains no storage plugin.
  * pve-syno-api-probe: read-only discovery of a Synology DSM's SAN Web API.
    Discovers API paths and version ranges rather than hardcoding them, carries
    a session both ways because the two public reference clients disagree about
    which works, echoes the anti-CSRF token neither of them sends, and never
    puts a credential in a URL.
  * docs/TESTING.md: the register of what is verified against hardware and what
    is not, with the test plan for closing the gap.
  * docs/DSM-ACCOUNT.md: the DSM account the plugin needs, what can be taken
    away from it, and the Auto Block behaviour that makes retrying a rejected
    credential dangerous.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0800
